#!/usr/bin/env ruby

# **Why a single `rspec` run is not enough for this class of bug**: every spec
# below proves a thread-safety property by forcing one specific
# interleaving (a `Queue`-based rendezvous, a `Mutex`-guarded "first
# arrival wins" gate) — real, but only as good as the one schedule the
# Ruby vm/OS actually hands it on a given run. A race that needs, say,
# thread A's `Thread.current[...]` write to land between thread B's read
# and its own write can pass every single time on a quiet CI box with two
# threads and no contention, and still be exactly the M20 bug
# (dispatcher.rb's `#reenter`, fixed by moving `@reaction_depth` off a
# plain ivar onto `Thread.current`) if a third thread happens to be
# running at the same time, or the scheduler happens to preempt at a
# different instruction. Gvl-level thread scheduling in CircleCI/GitHub
# Actions containers is also famously more deterministic (often
# effectively serialized under light load) than a real multi-core Puma
# worker pool under production traffic — the exact gap that let M20 ship
# in the first place despite `dispatcher_spec.rb`'s own sibling specs
# passing green on every PR.
#
# **This script's answer**: run the same specs many times, each run with a
# different random seed (RSpec's own `--seed` reorders example execution,
# which — combined with Ruby's own green-thread scheduler — changes which
# thread gets CPU time when) and, by default, several copies running
# concurrently as separate OS processes (`--parallel`), so the box is
# under genuine CPU/scheduler contention while each copy's own threads
# race — closer to "several Puma workers under load" than "one lonely
# rspec process with two threads and nothing else to do."
#
# Not a replacement for the specs themselves — a script that finds nothing
# new here proves only "not in THIS many tries," never "impossible." See
# the bottom of this file for how this is meant to be wired into CI: a
# repeat/matrix step on PRs touching lib/hecks/runtime/, not a replacement
# for the specs running normally on every PR.

require "etc"
require "open3"
require "fileutils"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# **The known thread-safety specs** — not "every spec that happens to use
# `Thread.new`", but specifically the ones proving a shared-mutable-state
# race is actually closed:
#
#   dispatcher_spec.rb                     — M20 itself (`@reaction_depth`
#                                             as a plain ivar vs.
#                                             `Thread.current`), a pure
#                                             in-process reproduction with
#                                             no I/O at all.
#   in_process_concurrent_dispatch_spec.rb — `Runtime::AggregateLock`'s
#                                             per-key `Mutex` under real
#                                             concurrent `Thread`s against
#                                             one process-local `Registry`
#                                             (Heki + Memory adapters).
#   postgres_concurrent_dispatch_spec.rb   — the same invariant against a
#                                             real Postgres row from two
#                                             independent `Dispatcher`s
#                                             (`io: true` — self-skips via
#                                             `PostgresProbe.available?`
#                                             when no Postgres is
#                                             reachable, so this script
#                                             works with or without one).
#
# Discovered by a fixed list, not a glob — unlike bin/fuzz's domain sweep,
# "every spec file that touches Thread" would catch plenty of ordinary
# fixture setup that spins up a thread for unrelated reasons and prove
# nothing about races. A glob here would trade a short, legible list for
# noise.
# Split in two, not one list — discovered the hard way running this
# script's own first draft: `postgres_concurrent_dispatch_spec.rb` targets
# one hardcoded database name (`DATABASE = "hecks_concurrency_gap_spec"`)
# that its own `before(:all)` drops and recreates. That's fine for a
# single `rspec` process — but run several `--parallel` copies of this
# script's own batch at once against a real, reachable Postgres and they
# all fight over that same physical database: one copy's `before(:all)`
# calling `DROP DATABASE ... WITH (FORCE)` while another copy is
# mid-transaction against it is a real `PG::ConnectionBad` ("server closed
# the connection unexpectedly") that has nothing to do with the
# application-level race the spec is actually proving — a false alarm
# from this script's own harness, not a finding. The Gemfile's own comment
# on `parallel_tests` already named this exact gap ("parallelizing
# [the io: true specs] against [Postgres] needs per-worker database naming
# this project doesn't have yet") before this script ever existed to
# rediscover it.
#
# So: the two in-process specs (no shared external resource — pure
# `Thread`s against one in-memory `Registry`) are genuinely safe to run as
# several concurrent OS processes, and get the real `--parallel` treatment
# below. The Postgres spec runs its own repeat loop one process at a time,
# always — still repeated `runs` times with a varied `--seed` each time
# (which does exercise different interleavings inside that one process's
# own two `Dispatcher`s), just never overlapped with a copy of itself.
PARALLEL_SAFE_SPEC_FILES = [
  "spec/runtime/dispatcher_spec.rb",
  "spec/adapters/driven/in_process_concurrent_dispatch_spec.rb"
].freeze

SERIAL_ONLY_SPEC_FILES = [
  "spec/adapters/driven/postgres_concurrent_dispatch_spec.rb"
].freeze

runs           = 30
parallel       = Etc.nprocessors
seed_start     = 1
failures_dir   = File.join(ROOT, "tmp/stress-failures")

until ARGV.empty?
  case (arg = ARGV.shift)
  when "--runs"       then runs = Integer(ARGV.shift)
  when "--parallel"   then parallel = Integer(ARGV.shift)
  when "--seed-start" then seed_start = Integer(ARGV.shift)
  when "--help", "-h"
    puts <<~USAGE
      Usage: bin/stress_concurrency_specs [--runs N] [--parallel N] [--seed-start N]

        --runs N        How many times to run EACH group below (default 30).
                         Each run uses a different --seed (seed-start + run
                         index).
        --parallel N    How many parallel-safe-group runs to have going as
                         separate OS processes AT THE SAME TIME (default: this
                         machine's own core count, via Etc.nprocessors) — real
                         concurrent scheduler/CPU contention, not just varied
                         seeds one after another. The Postgres-backed group
                         always runs one process at a time regardless of this
                         flag — see this file's own comment on why.
        --seed-start N  First --seed value (default 1); runs use seed_start,
                         seed_start + 1, ... seed_start + runs - 1.

      Exits 0 if every run's every example passed, 1 if anything failed —
      failing runs' full rspec output is saved under tmp/stress-failures/
      for a real repro (same seed, same command, just add CI=true).
    USAGE
    exit 0
  else
    warn "unrecognized argument: #{arg.inspect} (--help for usage)"
    exit 64
  end
end

# `CI=true` — spec_helper.rb only un-excludes `io: true` examples (the
# Postgres spec above) when `ENV["CI"]` is set or `--tag io` is passed
# explicitly; setting it here means this script exercises all three specs
# by default the same way a real CI run would, while the Postgres one
# still self-skips cleanly via `PostgresProbe.available?` if this machine
# has no reachable Postgres — no separate flag needed to opt in or out.
def run_once(spec_files, seed, label:)
  env = { "CI" => "true" }
  cmd = ["bundle", "exec", "rspec", *spec_files, "--seed", seed.to_s, "--format", "progress"]
  stdout, status = Open3.capture2e(env, *cmd, chdir: ROOT)
  { label: label, seed: seed, success: status.success?, output: stdout,
    reproduce: "CI=true bundle exec rspec #{spec_files.join(' ')} --seed #{seed}" }
end

def run_batch(spec_files, seeds, label:)
  # One thread per rspec process in this batch, not `Process.spawn`
  # directly — `Open3.capture2e` already spawns a real child process per
  # call (a genuinely separate OS process, its own Ruby vm, its own gvl),
  # so running several of those calls concurrently from Ruby `Thread`s is
  # enough to have `parallel` real processes running at once; the
  # coordinating threads here are just waiting on `Process.wait`
  # underneath `capture2e`, not doing any of the actual work themselves.
  seeds.map { |seed| Thread.new { run_once(spec_files, seed, label: label) } }.map(&:value)
end

puts "Stress-running the parallel-safe group #{runs} time(s) (#{parallel} at a time), " \
     "seeds #{seed_start}..#{seed_start + runs - 1}:"
PARALLEL_SAFE_SPEC_FILES.each { |f| puts "  - #{f}" }
puts "...and the Postgres-backed group #{runs} time(s), ONE PROCESS AT A TIME:"
SERIAL_ONLY_SPEC_FILES.each { |f| puts "  - #{f}" }
puts

results = []

(seed_start...(seed_start + runs)).each_slice(parallel) do |batch|
  batch_results = run_batch(PARALLEL_SAFE_SPEC_FILES, batch, label: "parallel-safe")
  results.concat(batch_results)
  batch_results.each { |result| print result[:success] ? "." : "F" }
  $stdout.flush
end

(seed_start...(seed_start + runs)).each do |seed|
  result = run_once(SERIAL_ONLY_SPEC_FILES, seed, label: "postgres (serial)")
  results << result
  print result[:success] ? "." : "F"
  $stdout.flush
end

puts
puts

failures = results.reject { |r| r[:success] }

if failures.empty?
  puts "CLEAN — #{results.size}/#{results.size} runs passed. " \
       "No new flakiness beyond a single ordinary `rspec` run found in this many tries."
  exit 0
end

FileUtils.mkdir_p(failures_dir)
puts "FOUND FLAKINESS — #{failures.size}/#{results.size} runs failed:"
failures.each do |failure|
  path = File.join(failures_dir, "#{failure[:label].tr(' ', '_').gsub(/[()]/, '')}-seed#{failure[:seed]}.log")
  File.write(path, failure[:output])
  puts "  [#{failure[:label]}] seed #{failure[:seed]} — output saved to #{path.sub("#{ROOT}/", '')}"
  puts "    reproduce: #{failure[:reproduce]}"
end
puts
exit 1

__END__

HOW TO WIRE THIS INTO CI (not done here — see this repo's own note on not
editing .github/workflows/ci.yml until a final integration pass; this is
the runnable piece that pass is meant to call):

  - Add a job that runs `bin/stress_concurrency_specs` (defaults: 30 runs,
    Etc.nprocessors at a time) gated to PRs touching `lib/hecks/runtime/**`
    — e.g. a `paths:` filter on the job, or a cheap `git diff
    --name-only origin/main... | grep -q '^lib/hecks/runtime/'` guard step
    that skips the rest of the job when it doesn't match.
  - Keep it SEPARATE from the main `bundle exec rspec` job rather than
    folded into it — this job's whole point is repeating a small, known
    subset many times, which takes meaningfully longer per-PR than the
    single-pass suite; gating it to the paths that can actually regress
    this class of bug is what keeps that cost worth paying on every
    relevant PR without taxing every unrelated one.
  - A real GitHub Actions matrix (`strategy: matrix: run: [1, 2, ..., N]`)
    is the OTHER standard way to get repetition-with-varied-scheduling in
    CI, and composes fine with this script too (each matrix leg just runs
    `bin/stress_concurrency_specs --runs 1 --parallel 1 --seed-start
    ${{ matrix.run }}`) — this script's own `--runs`/`--parallel` exist so
    the same tool also works for a developer's local loop before ever
    reaching for CI-side parallelism, not as a replacement for it.
